'''DeAndre Cortez Way''' (born July 28, 1990),<ref name="Reid-2009" /><ref name="EOnline-2009" /> better known by his stage name '''Soulja Boy''' (formerly '''Soulja Boy Tell 'Em'''), is an American rapper. He rose to prominence with his self-released 2007 debut single, "[[Crank That (Soulja Boy)]]", which peaked atop the US [[Billboard Hot 100|''Billboard'' Hot 100]] for seven non-consecutive weeks.<ref>{{Cite magazine |last=HipHopDX |date=November 12, 2008 |title=Soulja Boy, Will.i.am, Akon Take YouTube Live |url=http://www.hiphopdx.com/index/news/id.8073/title.soulja-boy-will-i-am-akon-take-youtube-live |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130818033707/http://www.hiphopdx.com/index/news/id.8073/title.soulja-boy-will-i-am-akon-ametake-youtube-live |archive-date=August 18, 2013 |access-date=December 17, 2009 |magazine=HipHopDX}}</ref> After a commercial re-release by [[Mr. Collipark|Collipark Music]], an imprint of [[Interscope Records]], the song and its follow-up, "[[Soulja Girl]]", both preceded his debut studio album, ''[[Souljaboytellem.com]]'' (2007). While critical reception was generally negative, the album peaked at number four on the US [[Billboard 200|''Billboard'' 200]] and spawned the single "[[Yahhh!]]"

His second album, ''[[iSouljaBoyTellem]]'' (2008)—supported by the ''Billboard'' Hot 100-top 20 singles "[[Turn My Swag On]]" and "[[Kiss Me thru the Phone]]" (featuring [[Sammie]])—was met with moderate commercial reception and a further decline in critical reception. Way was listed at number 18 on the ''[[Forbes]]'' list of Hip-Hop Cash Kings of 2010, as he earned US$7 million that year.<ref>{{Cite web |date=August 17, 2010 |title=Forbes List The 2010 Hip-Hop Cash Kings (Jay-Z Remains King Of Kings) |url=http://realtalkny.uproxx.com/2010/08/topic/topic/featured/forbes-list-the-2010-hip-hop-cash-kings-jay-z-remains-king-of-kings/ |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110521095001/http://realtalkny.uproxx.com/2010/08/topic/topic/featured/forbes-list-the-2010-hip-hop-cash-kings-jay-z-remains-king-of-kings/ |archive-date=May 21, 2011 |access-date=May 7, 2011 |publisher=Uproxx}}</ref> His third album, ''[[The DeAndre Way]]'' (2010)—supported by the top 40 single "[[Pretty Boy Swag]]"—saw a steeper decline in commercial reception, leading him to part ways with Interscope in favor of an [[independent music|independent]] career.

Way is noted for his pioneering use of grassroots [[social media marketing|social media]] and [[digital marketing]] strategies during his mainstream popularity.<ref>{{Cite web |date=December 16, 2016 |title=Soulja Boy is the OG Growth Hacker |url=https://www.entrepreneur.com/growing-a-business/soulja-boy-is-the-og-growth-hacker/286605}}</ref> Outside of recording, he has pursued side ventures including [[#SouljaGame|video game development]], [[Soulja Boy discography#Production discography|record production]] for other artists, and his record label [[#Other ventures|Stacks on Deck Entertainment]], through which he has signed rappers including [[Lil B]] and [[Riff Raff (rapper)|Riff Raff]]. He has received a [[Grammy Award]] nomination, as well as three [[BET Award]] and four [[Teen Choice Award]] nominations.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Albums That Killed the Artist's Career |url=https://www.gosocial.co/albums-that-killed-the-artists-career/}}</ref>

==Early life==
Way was born in [[Chicago]], Illinois, and at age six moved to [[Atlanta]], Georgia,<ref name="Cordor-2007">{{Cite web |last=Cordor |first=Cyril |year=2007 |title=Soulja Boy Tell Em - Biography |url={{AllMusic|class=artist|id=p984247|pure_url=yes}} |website=Allmusic}}</ref> where he became interested in [[rap music]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Soulja-Boy's SoundClick page |url=http://www.soundclick.com/members/default.cfm?member=Soulja-Boy |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130817234945/http://www.soundclick.com/members/default.cfm?member=Soulja-Boy |archive-date=August 17, 2013 |access-date=October 18, 2007 |website=soundclick.com}}</ref> At age 14, he moved to [[Batesville, Mississippi]], with his father.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Soulja Boy |url=https://www.bet.com/tag/9sofyf/soulja-boy |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211030010516/https://www.bet.com/tag/9sofyf/soulja-boy |url-status=dead |archive-date=October 30, 2021 |access-date=August 22, 2021 |website=BET.com}}</ref>

==Musical career==
===2004–2007: Early recordings and building an Internet following===
Way's father provided a [[recording studio]] for Way to explore his musical ambitions.<ref name="Cordor-2007" /> In early 2005, he was a frequent poster on the website [[Newgrounds]],<ref>{{Cite web |title=SouljaBoy |url=https://souljaboy.newgrounds.com/ |access-date=2024-05-04 |website=Newgrounds.com |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web | title=x.com | url=https://twitter.com/souljaboy/status/204956603710050304?lang=en | access-date=2024-12-25 | website=twitter.com}}</ref> and in November that same year, Way posted on the music website [[SoundClick]]. Following positive reviews on the site, he established accounts on [[YouTube]] and [[Myspace]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Soulja Boy |url=https://www.youtube.com/SouljaBoy |via=YouTube}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Featured Content on Myspace |url=https://www.myspace.com/souljaboytellem |access-date=July 20, 2015 |website=Myspace}}</ref> In March 2007, he released his first independent album, ''[[Unsigned and Still Major: Da Album Before Da Album]]''.

===2007–2011: ''Souljaboytellem.com'', ''iSouljaBoyTellem'', and ''The DeAndre Way''===
[[File:Soulja Boy.jpg|thumb|right|220px|Soulja Boy performing "[[Crank That (Soulja Boy)]]" in February 2008.|alt=]]
Also in March 2007, Way recorded "[[Crank That (Soulja Boy)]]", followed by a low-budget video demonstrating the "Crank That" dance. On May 2, "Crank That" was released as a single; by month's end, it had received its first airplay, and Way met with [[Mr. Collipark]] to sign a recording contract with his record label Collipark Music, which later entered a joint venture with [[Interscope Records]] shortly after.<ref>{{Cite web |date=December 1, 2009 |title=Soulja Boy Turns His Swag On – Our Soulja Boy Interview |url=https://parlemag.com/interviews/artists-interviews/soulja-boy-turns-his-swag-on/ |website=Parlemag.com}}</ref> On August 12, "Crank That" was used on the television series ''[[Entourage (U.S. TV series)|Entourage]]'', and on September 1 it topped the US [[Billboard Hot 100|''Billboard'' Hot 100]] and [[Billboard charts#Ringtones|Hot RingMasters]] charts.<ref>Vibe Magazine. Ayo, Technology by Brent S. Grier. November 2007. Page 50.</ref> During the [[50th Annual Grammy Awards]], "Crank That (Soulja Boy)" was nominated for [[Grammy Award for Best Rap Song|Best Rap Song]], losing to [[Kanye West]]'s and [[T-Pain]]'s "[[Good Life (Kanye West song)|Good Life]]".

Way's major label debut album, ''[[Souljaboytellem.com]]'', was released in the United States on October 2,<ref name="Jeffries-2007">Jeffries, David. [{{AllMusic|class=album|id=r1209589|pure_url=yes}} "Souljaboytellem.com" Overview]. [[Allmusic]]: 2007</ref> peaking at number four on both the [[Billboard 200|''Billboard'' 200]] and [[Top R&B/Hip-Hop Albums]] charts.<ref name="Jeffries-2007"/> The album was reportedly recorded using just the demo version of [[FL Studio]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=SoundClick artist: Soulja Boy - Soulja Boy Tell 'Em - Interscope Records in Stores now! |url=http://www.soundclick.com/bands/default.cfm?bandID=372975 |website=SoundClick}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Team OnSMASH |date=September 13, 2007 |title=50 Cent & Soulja Boy |url=http://www.dailymotion.com/teamonsmash/video/x2ysmb_50-cent-soulja-boy_fun |website=Dailymotion}}</ref> ''Souljaboytellem.com'' received a favorable review from [[Allmusic]]<ref name="Jeffries-2007"/> but mainly negative reviews from other sources, such as ''[[Entertainment Weekly]]''.<ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Vozick-Levinson |first=Simon |date=October 5, 2007 |title=Music Review: Souljaboytellem.com |url=http://www.ew.com/ew/article/0,,20144964,00.html |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131111132704/http://www.ew.com/ew/article/0,,20144964,00.html |archive-date=November 11, 2013 |access-date=December 27, 2007 |magazine=Entertainment Weekly |quote="Crank That" is diverting enough for three and a half minutes, but most of his debut, ''Souljaboytellem.com'', is ''a teenage wasteland filled with monotonously looped chants and agonizing blunt-force beats''. At least he owns a cool domain name. '''D'''}}</ref> Several reviewers credited Soulja Boy with spearheading a new trend in hip-hop, while speculating that he would be a [[one-hit wonder]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Robinson |first=Patrick |title=Soulja Boy - Souljaboytellem.com Review |url=http://www.411mania.com/music/album_reviews/60943 |access-date=December 27, 2007 |publisher=411mania.com, LLC |quote=...{{nbsp}}not a chore to listen to in that sense and Soulja Boy does show some signs of youthful enthusiasm.}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Griff |title=Soulja Boy - Souljaboytellem.com |url=http://www.okayplayer.com/reviews/index.php/weblog/more/souljaboytellemcom/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20071225015537/http://www.okayplayer.com/reviews/index.php/weblog/more/souljaboytellemcom/ <!-- Bot retrieved archive --> |archive-date=December 25, 2007 |access-date=December 27, 2007 |website=Okayplayer.com |quote=This one's definitely geared for the kiddies{{nbsp}}... does an excellent job of catering to current industry trends with ringtone themes{{nbsp}}... and dance moves galore{{nbsp}}... Despite all the criticism that he's received from hip-hop purists, it'd be naive to think Soulja Boy hasn't changed the face of hip-hop}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=''The Review'' - Review of ''Souljaboytellem.com'' |url=http://media.www.udreview.com/media/storage/paper781/news/2007/10/02/Mosaic/Soulja.Boy.Tell.em.souljaboytellem.com.Reviewed-3004601.shtml |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090715040359/http://media.www.udreview.com/media/storage/paper781/news/2007/10/02/Mosaic/Soulja.Boy.Tell.em.souljaboytellem.com.Reviewed-3004601.shtml |archive-date=July 15, 2009 |access-date=June 11, 2016}}</ref>

The follow-up to ''Souljaboytellem.com'', ''[[iSouljaBoyTellem]]'', was released on December 16, 2008, to negative critical reception.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Darryl Sterdan |date=December 15, 2008 |title=Reviewing the final new CDs of 2008; Soulja Boy Tell Em |publisher=Winnipeg Sun (Manitoba) |quote=It isn't easy being Soulja. Everybody from Ice-T to LeBron James has been hating on the 18-year-old rapper and his hit Crank That. But before you feel sorry for him, crank his second CD. Then again, don't. Between the tossed-off shallowness of his tracks and the monotony of his repetitive vocals, you might decide the only thing worse than being Soulja Boy is listening to him}}</ref> The first single from the album, "[[Bird Walk]]", peaked at number 40 on the ''Billboard'' [[Hot R&B/Hip-Hop Songs]] chart and the top 20 on the [[Hot Rap Songs|Hot Rap Tracks]] chart. It was performed on [[YouTube Live]] on November 22, 2008, with an introduction from [[MC Hammer]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=November 22, 2008 |title=Soulja Boy Tell 'Em - YouTube Live Performance |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0puX3pHhGMc |access-date=May 7, 2011 |via=YouTube}}</ref> "[[Kiss Me thru the Phone|Kiss Me Thru the Phone]]" (featuring [[Sammie]]) followed, peaking at number three on the Hot 100 and number one on the Hot Rap Tracks charts.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Soulja Boy Tell Em - Charts and Awards - Billboard Singles |url={{AllMusic|class=artist|id=p984247|pure_url=yes}} |access-date=February 18, 2009 |publisher=allmusic}}</ref> [[Chris Brown]] was Way's first choice to sing the hook, but respectfully turned down the offer. Way then tried to do it on his own with [[Auto-Tune]] but found it unacceptable.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Westhoff |first=Ben |date=August 19, 2009 |title=Dallas Music - The Five Most Interesting Things You Didn't Know About Soulja Boy - page 1 |url=http://www.dallasobserver.com/2009-08-20/music/the-five-most-interesting-things-you-didn-t-know-about-soulja-boy/ |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100526184836/http://www.dallasobserver.com/2009-08-20/music/the-five-most-interesting-things-you-didn-t-know-about-soulja-boy/ |archive-date=May 26, 2010 |access-date=August 20, 2009 |website=[[Dallas Observer]]}}</ref> The song sold over 2 million digital copies in the U.S., becoming Way's second to reach two million downloads.<ref>[http://new.music.yahoo.com/blogs/chart_watch/33675/week-ending-may-17-2009-three-days-is-plenty-for-green-day/ Week Ending May 17, 2009: Three Days Is Plenty For Green Day] Yahoo Music Blog (written by Paul Grein): Retrieved May 20, 2009</ref><ref name="Grein-2009">{{Cite web |last=Grein |first=Paul |title=Week Ending Aug. 23, 2009: Over 50 And Still On Top |url=http://new.music.yahoo.com/blogs/chart_watch/41066/week-ending-aug-23-2009-over-50-and-still-on-top/ |access-date=August 26, 2009 |publisher=[[Yahoo! Music]]}}</ref> On January 26, 2009, Way released the third single, "[[Turn My Swag On]]". It topped the U.S. rap charts and peaked at number 19 on the ''Billboard'' Hot 100. {{As of|2009|08|23|df=US}}, it has sold more than 1 million digital downloads in the U.S.<ref name="Grein-2009" />

[[File:Soulja Boy Tell 'Em on YouTube Live.jpg|thumb|left|180px|Soulja Boy at [[YouTube Live]] in November 2008.]]
Way said his third studio album, ''[[The DeAndre Way (album)|The DeAndre Way]]'',<ref>{{Cite web |last=Harper |first=Rosario |date=August 26, 2010 |title=Soulja Boy Tell Em Suffers "DeAndre Way" Setback |url=http://www.sohh.com/2010/08/soulja_boy_tell_em_suffers_deandre_way_s.html |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100829073721/http://www.sohh.com/2010/08/soulja_boy_tell_em_suffers_deandre_way_s.html |archive-date=August 29, 2010 |access-date=May 7, 2011 |publisher=Sohh.Com}}</ref> was intended to be his most personal and successful album thus far. He said he wished to collaborate with artists such as [[Jay-Z]], [[Lil Wayne]], [[Kanye West]]<ref>{{Cite web |title=Soulja Boy Tell'em Wants Jay-Z, Lil Wayne On DeAndre Way |url=http://www.mtv.com/news/articles/1611119/20090511/soulja_boy_tellem.jhtml |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121104090538/http://www.mtv.com/news/articles/1611119/soulja-boy-wants-jay-z-lil-wayne-on-next-lp.jhtml |archive-date=November 4, 2012 |website=MTV News}}</ref> and [[Eminem]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=March 15, 2010 |title=Soulja Boy Speaks on New Album "Dre" // Current |url=http://current.com/entertainment/music/92321813_soulja-boy-speaks-on-new-album-dre.htm |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121003081207/http://current.com/entertainment/music/92321813_soulja-boy-speaks-on-new-album-dre.htm |archive-date=October 3, 2012 |access-date=May 7, 2011 |publisher=Current.com}}</ref> The album's lead single, "POW", was released in January 2009 but failed to garner success and was dubbed a promo single. On October 30, 2009, Way released three mixtapes: ''Paranormal Activity'', ''Dat Piff'' and ''Cortez''.<ref>{{Cite web |last=RealTalkNY |date=October 30, 2009 |title=New Soulja Boy Mixtapes &#124; RealTalkNY |url=http://realtalkny.uproxx.com/2009/10/topic/topic/music/new-soulja-boy-mixtapes/ |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110725103222/http://realtalkny.uproxx.com/2009/10/topic/topic/music/new-soulja-boy-mixtapes/ |archive-date=July 25, 2011 |access-date=May 7, 2011 |publisher=Realtalkny.uproxx.com}}</ref> It was announced on February 18, 2010, that the first single from the album was going to be "All Black Everything", but the single was canceled and the track was put on another promotional mixtape, ''Legendary''.<ref>{{Cite web |last=RealTalkNY |date=February 27, 2010 |title=Soulja Boy – Legendary Mixtape &#124; RealTalkNY |url=http://realtalkny.uproxx.com/2010/02/topic/topic/music/soulja-boy-legendary-mixtape/ |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110725102903/http://realtalkny.uproxx.com/2010/02/topic/topic/music/soulja-boy-legendary-mixtape/ |archive-date=July 25, 2011 |access-date=May 7, 2011 |publisher=Realtalkny.uproxx.com}}</ref> The album's title was changed to ''Dre'' in an early 2010 commercial with a video snippet for "Do It Big" as the first single, but this was also canceled.<ref>{{Cite web |last=RealTalkNY |date=April 5, 2010 |title=Commercial For Soulja Boy's New Album, "Dre" &#124; RealTalkNY |url=http://realtalkny.uproxx.com/2010/04/topic/topic/videos/commercial-for-soulja-boys-new-album-dre/ |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110725102951/http://realtalkny.uproxx.com/2010/04/topic/topic/videos/commercial-for-soulja-boys-new-album-dre/ |archive-date=July 25, 2011 |access-date=May 7, 2011 |publisher=Realtalkny.uproxx.com}}</ref> The album title reverted to ''The DeAndre Way'' in July 2010.
On June 8, 2010, the album's official lead single was "[[Pretty Boy Swag]]". It reached number 34 on the ''Billboard'' Hot 100, number six on the Hot R&B/Hip-Hop Songs chart and number five on the Rap Songs chart.<ref>[{{BillboardURLbyName|artist=soulja boy tell em|chart=all}} Artist Chart History - Soulja Boy Tell Em] Billboard.com</ref> The album's second single was due to be "Digital", which was instead used on his promotional mixtape ''Best Rapper''.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Soulja Boy - Best Rapper - DJ Woogie, Evil Empire |url=http://www.livemixtapes.com/mixtapes/12037/soulja_boy_best_rapper.html |access-date=May 7, 2011 |publisher=Livemixtapes.com}}</ref> Then it was announced that "[[Speakers Going Hammer]]" was going to be the second single, but "[[Blowing Me Kisses]]" was released on August 31, 2010, as the second single instead.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Rodriguez |first=Jayson |date=July 27, 2010 |title=Soulja Boy Tell'em Prepping Second DeAndre Way Single, 'Speakers Going Hammer' News |url=http://www.mtv.com/news/articles/1644479/20100726/soulja_boy_tellem.jhtml |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100729121841/http://www.mtv.com/news/articles/1644479/20100726/soulja_boy_tellem.jhtml |archive-date=July 29, 2010 |access-date=May 7, 2011 |publisher=MTV}}</ref> On October 13, Way performed "Pretty Boy Swag" and a snippet of "Speakers Going Hammer". On October 19, 2010, he released "Speakers Going Hammer" on iTunes; it peaked at number 48 on the ''Billboard'' Hot R&B/Hip Hop Songs.<ref>{{Cite web |last=RealTalkNY |date=October 13, 2010 |title=BET Hip Hop Awards Performances: Rick Ross, Dipset, J. Cole, Antoine Dodson and More &#124; RealTalkNY |url=http://realtalkny.uproxx.com/2010/10/topic/topic/videos/bet-hip-hop-awards-performances-rick-ross-dipset-j-cole-antoine-dodson-more/ |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110725103052/http://realtalkny.uproxx.com/2010/10/topic/topic/videos/bet-hip-hop-awards-performances-rick-ross-dipset-j-cole-antoine-dodson-more/ |archive-date=July 25, 2011 |access-date=May 7, 2011 |publisher=Realtalkny.uproxx.com}}</ref> Way backed out of the [[Summerbeatz]] tour in Australia in November 2010, where he was to have performed alongside [[Flo Rida]], [[Jay Sean]] and [[Travie McCoy]],<ref>{{Cite web |title=Ja Rule Replaces Soulja Boy At Summerbeatz |url=http://www.take40.com/news/20885/ja-rule-replaces-soulja-boy-at-summerbeatz |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20101222114009/http://www.take40.com/news/20885/ja-rule-replaces-soulja-boy-at-summerbeatz |archive-date=December 22, 2010 |access-date=November 17, 2010 |publisher=[[Take40 Australia]]}}</ref> in order to embark on his "Who They Want" tour in support of his upcoming album. ''The DeAndre Way'' was released on November 30, 2010, and has so far{{when|date=August 2013}} sold only 70,000 copies,<!--lead says 100k copies--> making it Way's lowest-selling album.

===2011–2018: Mixtapes, EPs, independent albums, and ''Loyalty''===
In 2011, Way released a series of mixtapes. The first, on January 22, was titled ''Smooky'' and had cartoon cover art of Way doing a 360 on a bicycle.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Soulja Boy - Smooky // Free Mixtape @ |url=http://www.datpiff.com/Soulja-Boy-Smooky-mixtape.191718.html |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110426025756/http://www.datpiff.com/Soulja-Boy-Smooky-mixtape.191718.html |archive-date=April 26, 2011 |access-date=May 7, 2011 |publisher=Datpiff.com}}</ref> On March 17, Way released the mixtape ''1UP'' which had cover art based on the ''[[Naruto]]'' [[anime]], with Way stating: "If I was in a video game, this mixtape would advance me to the next level with an extra life. Power up with the new offering."<ref>{{Cite web |date=March 17, 2011 |title=New Music: Soulja Boy – '1UP' [Mixtape&#93; |url=http://www.rap-up.com/2011/03/17/new-music-soulja-boy-1up-mixtape/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110321014811/http://www.rap-up.com/2011/03/17/new-music-soulja-boy-1up-mixtape |url-status=dead |archive-date=March 21, 2011 |access-date=May 7, 2011 |publisher=Rap-Up.com}}</ref> On April 20, Way released his mixtape ''Juice'' with cover art based on the [[Juice (1992 film)|film of the same name]]. Way said he would also release a mini-movie of his own to go along with the mixtape, and that he was headed to greatness with the mixtape.<ref>{{Cite magazine |date=April 20, 2011 |title=Soulja Boy: 'I'm Headed for Greatness' with 'Juice' Mixtape, Mini-Movie - The Juice |url=https://www.billboard.com/music/music-news/soulja-boy-im-headed-for-greatness-with-juice-mixtape-mini-movie-471969/ |access-date=May 7, 2011 |magazine=Billboard}}</ref> On July 15, Way released his {{Abbr|EP|extended play}} mixtape titled ''Bernaurd Arnault EP (Limited Edition)''.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Soulja Boy - Bernard Arnault EP (Limited Edition) Hosted by SODMG |url=http://www.datpiff.com/Soulja-Boy-Bernard-Arnault-EP-Limited-Edition-mixtape.251283.html |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121027123601/http://www.datpiff.com/Soulja-Boy-Bernard-Arnault-EP-Limited-Edition-mixtape.251283.html |archive-date=October 27, 2012 |access-date=September 6, 2012 |website=DatPiff}}</ref> Then on August 1, Way released another ep mixtape titled ''21: EP''.<ref>{{Cite web |date=August 1, 2011 |title=Soulja Boy - 21: EP Hosted by SODMG |url=http://www.datpiff.com/Soulja-Boy-21-EP-mixtape.257596.html |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170301133253/http://www.datpiff.com/Soulja-Boy-21-EP-mixtape.257596.html |archive-date=March 1, 2017 |access-date=November 22, 2016 |website=DatPiff}}</ref> On August 31, Way released the mixtape ''The Last Crown'', the cover of the mixtape is based on the artwork the [[Flammarion engraving|Flammarion Engraving]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Andrew Noz |title=Soulja Boy's The Last Crown Mixtape |url=http://www.thefader.com/2011/09/01/soulja-boys-the-last-crown-mixtape/ |website=The FADER}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Soulja Boy - The Last Crown Hosted by SODMG |url=http://www.datpiff.com/Soulja-Boy-The-Last-Crown-mixtape.262854.html |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121005092249/http://www.datpiff.com/Soulja-Boy-The-Last-Crown-mixtape.262854.html |archive-date=October 5, 2012 |access-date=September 6, 2012 |website=DatPiff}}</ref> In September 2011, Way confirmed that he is working on a new album, ''[[Loyalty (Soulja Boy album)|Promise]]'', which would be released in October.<ref>{{Cite web |last=SouljaBoy |date=September 16, 2011 |title=Soulja Boy - S.Beezy TV Webisodes: New Album part 1 |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n3X3cPZlWaQ |access-date=November 24, 2011 |via=YouTube}}</ref> Way also released the cover and title of his second independent album via Twitter, titled ''Skate Boy'', with a notice that it would be available in stores on November 1.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Volledige naam |title=Twitter |url=https://www.twitter.com/souljaboy/media/slideshow?url=pic.twitter.com%2FDqiH13n7 |access-date=March 22, 2013 |publisher=Twitter}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Angelo |date=October 1, 2011 |title=Soulja Boy – Skate Boy (Album Cover) &#124; HipHop-N-More |url=http://hiphop-n-more.com/2011/10/soulja-boy-skate-boy-album-cover/ |access-date=November 24, 2011 |publisher=Hiphop-n-more.com}}</ref> On September 19, Way released the mixtape ''Supreme''.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Soulja Boy (S.Beezy) - Supreme Hosted by SODMG |url=http://www.datpiff.com/Soulja-Boy-SBeezy-Supreme-mixtape.263011.html |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120719090416/http://www.datpiff.com/Soulja-Boy-SBeezy-Supreme-mixtape.263011.html |archive-date=July 19, 2012 |access-date=September 6, 2012 |website=DatPiff}}</ref> On October 30, ''Skate Boy (Deluxe Edition)'' was released as a mixtape, rather than an album.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Soulja Boy - Skate Boy (Deluxe Edition) Hosted by SODMG, Ocean Gang, Rich Kingz, Alien Team |url=http://www.datpiff.com/Soulja-Boy-Skate-Boy-Deluxe-Edition-mixtape.278041.html |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121027123625/http://www.datpiff.com/Soulja-Boy-Skate-Boy-Deluxe-Edition-mixtape.278041.html |archive-date=October 27, 2012 |access-date=September 5, 2012 |website=DatPiff}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Soulja Boy - Skate Boy |url=http://www.livemixtapes.com/mixtapes/15176/soulja_boy_skate_boy.html |website=LiveMixtapes}}</ref> On December 30, 2011, Way released another mixtape titled ''Gold On Deck''.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Soulja Boy - Gold On Deck |url=http://www.datpiff.com/Soulja-Boy-Gold-On-Deck-mixtape.298767.html |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121027123634/http://www.datpiff.com/Soulja-Boy-Gold-On-Deck-mixtape.298767.html |archive-date=October 27, 2012 |access-date=September 5, 2012 |website=DatPiff}}</ref>

In 2012, Way released additional mixtapes while his album continued to be delayed. On January 9, Way released a mixtape for the beginning of the year, titled ''50/13'', with the cover based on that of [[ASAP Rocky]]'s mixtape ''[[Live. Love. ASAP]]''.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Soulja Boy - 50/13 Hosted by SODMG Ent. |url=http://www.datpiff.com/Soulja-Boy-5013-mixtape.302427.html |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121027123643/http://www.datpiff.com/Soulja-Boy-5013-mixtape.302427.html |archive-date=October 27, 2012 |access-date=September 5, 2012 |website=DatPiff}}</ref> On January 24, Way released a collaboration mixtape with his artist Young L titled ''Mario & Domo vs. the World'' with cover art based on the video game ''[[Super Mario Bros.]]''<ref>{{Cite web |title=Soulja Boy and Young L - Mario & Domo Vs. The World Hosted by SODMG Ent. |url=http://www.datpiff.com/Soulja-Boy-Young-L-Mario-Domo-Vs-The-World-mixtape.307889.html |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121027123614/http://www.datpiff.com/Soulja-Boy-Young-L-Mario-Domo-Vs-The-World-mixtape.307889.html |archive-date=October 27, 2012 |access-date=September 5, 2012 |website=DatPiff}}</ref> On March 23, Way released a mixtape titled ''OBEY'' which featured the promotional single "Too Faded".<ref>{{Cite web |title=Soulja Boy - Obey Hosted by SODMG |url=http://www.datpiff.com/Soulja-Boy-Obey-mixtape.328307.html |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121027123638/http://www.datpiff.com/Soulja-Boy-Obey-mixtape.328307.html |archive-date=October 27, 2012 |access-date=September 5, 2012 |website=DatPiff}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Naomi Zeichner |title=Download Soulja Boy's Obey Mixtape |url=http://www.thefader.com/2012/03/23/download-soulja-boys-obey-mixtape/ |website=The FADER}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Too Faded (featuring The Game) - Single by Soulja Boy |url=https://music.apple.com/us/album/too-faded-feat-game-single/500906305 |access-date=November 5, 2011 |website=iTunes |publisher=Apple}}</ref> On June 3, 2012, Way talked about his upcoming album ''Promise'' and said he has released so many mixtapes in the last two years because he feels he has to put out a lot of music and be as creative as possible.<ref>{{Cite web |date=July 3, 2012 |title=Soulja Boy Discusses His Upcoming Album "Promise" |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jYaoKyPUoJM |via=YouTube}}</ref> On June 11, Way released another collaboration mixtape with rapper Vinny Chase titled ''Double Cup City''.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Soulja Boy & Vinny Cha$e - Double Cup City Hosted by SODMG |url=http://www.datpiff.com/Soulja-Boy-Vinny-Chae-Double-Cup-City-mixtape.361495.html |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120716042154/http://www.datpiff.com/Soulja-Boy-Vinny-Chae-Double-Cup-City-mixtape.361495.html |archive-date=July 16, 2012 |access-date=September 5, 2012 |website=DatPiff}}</ref> On September 4, Way released the sequel mixtape ''Juice II'', in which Way returned to his old production style with the beginning lines "Soulja Boy Tell 'Em". It is Way's most-downloaded mixtape.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Soulja Boy - Juice II Hosted by SODMG |url=http://www.datpiff.com/Soulja-Boy-Juice-II-mixtape.392026.html |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120905012324/http://www.datpiff.com/Soulja-Boy-Juice-II-mixtape.392026.html |archive-date=September 5, 2012 |access-date=September 5, 2012 |website=DatPiff}}</ref> His mixtape titled ''Young & Flexin'' was released on November 6.<ref>{{Cite web |date=November 5, 2012 |title=Mixtape Release Dates: Master P, Fashawn, Gunplay, Mickey Factz |url=http://www.hiphopdx.com/index/news/id.21739/title.mixtape-release-dates-master-p-fashawn-gunplay-mickey-factz |access-date=November 5, 2012}}</ref> On December 25, 2012, Way released ''LOUD'', which was his first mixtape distributed on iTunes.<ref>{{Cite web |date=December 25, 2012 |title=Soulja Boy - LOUD Hosted by SODMG // Free Mixtape @ |url=http://www.datpiff.com/Soulja-Boy-LOUD-mixtape.433673.html |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130301071234/http://www.datpiff.com/Soulja-Boy-LOUD-mixtape.433673.html |archive-date=March 1, 2013 |access-date=March 22, 2013 |publisher=Datpiff.com}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=December 26, 2012 |title=iTunes - Music - Loud by Soulja Boy Tell 'Em |url=https://itunes.apple.com/us/album/loud/id590911788 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130425082023/https://itunes.apple.com/us/album/loud/id590911788 |url-status=dead |archive-date=April 25, 2013 |access-date=March 22, 2013 |publisher=[[iTunes]]}}</ref>

2013 was marked by further album delays while Way's record deal expired, and he continued to release mixtapes. On February 22, Way released his first mixtape of the year, titled ''Foreign''.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Soulja Boy - Foreign Hosted by DJ Wats |url=http://www.datpiff.com/Soulja-Boy-Foreign-mixtape.458433.html |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130313103318/http://www.datpiff.com/Soulja-Boy-Foreign-mixtape.458433.html |archive-date=March 13, 2013 |access-date=March 27, 2013 |website=DatPiff}}</ref> In March, it was rumored that Way was signing to [[Cash Money Records]] after getting a face tattoo saying "Rich Gang" the same weekend he was seen with his manager and Cash Money artists [[Birdman (rapper)|Birdman]] and [[Bow Wow (rapper)|Bow Wow]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Starbury |first=Allen |date=March 12, 2013 |title=Soulja Boy To Sign With Cash Money? Gets 'Rich Gang' Face Tattoo |url=http://www.ballerstatus.com/2013/03/12/soulja-boy-sign-cash-money-rich-gang-face-tattoo/ |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://archive.today/20130410131355/http://www.ballerstatus.com/2013/03/12/soulja-boy-sign-cash-money-rich-gang-face-tattoo/ |archive-date=April 10, 2013 |access-date=March 22, 2013 |publisher=BallerStatus.com}}</ref> This continued after he added "Rich Gang" or "YMCMB" to his social media platforms. Way had earlier reported that he was no longer with Interscope Records after his record deal ended in February.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Did Soulja Boy Sign With YMCMB? |url=http://www.complex.com/music/2013/03/did-soulja-boy-sign-with-ymcmb |access-date=March 22, 2013 |publisher=Complex}}</ref> Way also announced that his album ''Promise'' had been retitled ''[[Loyalty (Soulja Boy album)|USA DRE]]''. On March 15, he released the first single from the album, titled "Handsome".<ref>{{Cite web |date=March 11, 2013 |title=Soulja Boy, "Handsome" |url=http://www.xxlmag.com/rap-music/bangers/2013/03/soulja-boy-handsome/ |website=XXL}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=March 15, 2013 |title=iTunes - Music - Handsome - Single by Soulja Boy Tell 'Em |url=https://music.apple.com/us/album/handsome-single/622611468 |website=iTunes}}</ref> Following that, on March 25, he released the EP, ''All Black''.<ref>{{Cite web |date=March 25, 2013 |title=iTunes - Music - All Black - EP by Soulja Boy Tell 'Em |url=https://music.apple.com/us/album/all-black-ep/626908611 |website=iTunes}}</ref> On April 24, Way released his mixtape ''Foreign 2''.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Soulja Boy - Foreign 2 Hosted by SODMG |url=http://www.datpiff.com/Soulja-Boy-Foreign-2-mixtape.481503.html |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130427001615/http://www.datpiff.com/Soulja-Boy-Foreign-2-mixtape.481503.html |archive-date=April 27, 2013 |access-date=April 26, 2013 |website=DatPiff}}</ref> Three days later, Way revealed the cover art to his next mixtape titled ''King Soulja'' which was released on May 5.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Nicolas James |date=April 28, 2013 |title=Soulja Boy Reveals New Mixtape Title, Cover Art and Release Date |url=http://www.hotnewhiphop.com/soulja-boy-reveals-new-mixtape-title-cover-art-and-release-date-news.5451.html |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130501075735/http://www.hotnewhiphop.com/soulja-boy-reveals-new-mixtape-title-cover-art-and-release-date-news.5451.html |archive-date=May 1, 2013 |access-date=April 28, 2013 |website=HotNewHipHop}}</ref> On May 26, Way released his second EP titled ''Cuban Link''.<ref>{{Cite web |date=May 27, 2013 |title=iTunes - Music - Cuban Link EP by Soulja Boy Tell 'Em |url=https://music.apple.com/us/album/cuban-link-ep/655015296 |website=iTunes}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Soulja Boy - Cuban Link EP Hosted by RichGang |url=http://www.datpiff.com/Soulja-Boy-Cuban-Link-EP-mixtape.492593.html |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130615123933/http://www.datpiff.com/Soulja-Boy-Cuban-Link-Ep-mixtape.492593.html |archive-date=June 15, 2013 |access-date=June 17, 2013 |website=DatPiff}}</ref> On June 13, Way announced another album title change, to ''[[Loyalty (Soulja Boy album)|Life After Fame]]'', and that it would be released to stores on July 30, 2013.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Soulja Boy on Twitter |url=https://twitter.com/souljaboy/status/347079145018257408 |website=Twitter}}</ref> On June 25, Way released his first single from the album titled "Ridin Round" to iTunes, but it was later dubbed a promotional single.<ref>{{Cite web |date=June 24, 2013 |title=Soulja Boy - Ridin Round |url=https://www.hotnewhiphop.com/546786-soulja-boy-ridin-round-song |website=HotNewHipHop}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Soulja Boy on Twitter |url=https://twitter.com/souljaboy/status/349213459805782016 |website=Twitter}}</ref> On June 30, Way released ''Life After Fame'' as a mixtape instead of an album.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Soulja Boy - Life After Fame |url=http://www.datpiff.com/Soulja-Boy-Life-After-Fame-mixtape.515477.html |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130801194402/http://www.datpiff.com/Soulja-Boy-Life-After-Fame-mixtape.515477.html |archive-date=August 1, 2013 |access-date=August 3, 2013 |website=DatPiff}}</ref> On September 24, Way released his mixtape titled ''23''.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Soulja Boy - 23 Hosted by Stacks On Deck |url=http://www.datpiff.com/Soulja-Boy-23-mixtape.534646.html |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130925155113/http://www.datpiff.com/Soulja-Boy-23-mixtape.534646.html |archive-date=September 25, 2013 |access-date=September 25, 2013 |website=DatPiff}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=September 24, 2013 |title=Soulja Boy - 23 |url=https://www.hotnewhiphop.com/453570-soulja-boy-23-the-mixtape-mixtape |website=HotNewHipHop}}</ref> On November 30, Way released his mixtape ''The King''.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Soulja Boy - The King Hosted by SODMG |url=http://www.datpiff.com/Soulja-Boy-The-King-mixtape.555926.html |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140209212019/http://www.datpiff.com/Soulja-Boy-The-King-mixtape.555926.html |archive-date=February 9, 2014 |access-date=March 2, 2014 |website=DatPiff}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=November 30, 2013 |title=Soulja Boy - The King |url=https://www.hotnewhiphop.com/453409-soulja-boy-the-king-mixtape |website=HotNewHipHop}}</ref> On December 28, 2013, rapper/singer [[Drake (rapper)|Drake]] collaborated and remixed Way's intro track "We Made It" from his mixtape ''The King''.<ref>{{Cite AV media |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RmBB_3sFNkQ%7ctitle=YouTube%7cwork=YouTube |title=YouTube |access-date=July 20, 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131228203556/http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RmBB_3sFNkQ&gl=US&hl=en |archive-date=December 28, 2013}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Kristobak |first=Ryan |date=December 28, 2013 |title=Drake Releases 'We Made It' Freestyle Over Soulja Boy Track |url=http://www.huffingtonpost.com/2013/12/28/drake-we-made-it_n_4512569.html |work=The Huffington Post}}</ref>

In 2014, Way continued work on his fourth studio album while collaborating with other artists and releasing additional mixtapes. On March 18, Way released his first mixtape of the year titled ''King Soulja 2''.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Soulja Boy - King Soulja 2 Hosted by SODMG |url=http://www.datpiff.com/Soulja-Boy-King-Soulja-2-mixtape.592105.html |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140319162103/http://www.datpiff.com/Soulja-Boy-King-Soulja-2-mixtape.592105.html |archive-date=March 19, 2014 |access-date=June 26, 2014 |website=DatPiff}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=March 18, 2014 |title=Soulja Boy - King Soulja 2 |url=https://www.hotnewhiphop.com/453503-soulja-boy-king-soulja-2-new-mixtape |website=HotNewHipHop}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Soulja Boy - King Soulja II |url=http://www.livemixtapes.com/mixtapes/26722/soulja-boy-king-soulja-ii.html |website=LiveMixtapes}}</ref> On April 20, Way released his first [[digital album]] ''Super Dope'', featuring an appearance from rapper [[Busta Rhymes]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=April 20, 2014 |title=iTunes - Music - Super Dope by Soulja Boy Tell 'Em |url=https://itunes.apple.com/us/album/super-dope/id863241714 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140709152834/https://itunes.apple.com/us/album/super-dope/id863241714 |url-status=dead |archive-date=July 9, 2014 |website=iTunes}}</ref> On May 19, Way produced, co-wrote and was featured on [[Nicki Minaj]]'s promotional single "[[Yasss Bish]]", which received positive reviews from music critics.<ref>{{Cite magazine |title=Soulja Boy talks being an emoji, working with Nicki for 'Yasss Bish' - EW.com |url=http://popwatch.ew.com/2014/05/12/soulja-boy-emoji-nicki-minaj-yasss-bish/ |magazine=Entertainment Weekly}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Nicki Minaj and Soulja Boy Collaborate On "Yasss Bish!!" |url=http://www.fuse.tv/2014/05/nicki-minaj-soulja-boy-yasss-bish |website=Fuse}}</ref> On June 8, Way announced that he was currently working on his new mixtape project ''King Soulja 3'', which would feature appearances from [[Lil Wayne]], [[Drake (rapper)|Drake]], [[Migos]], [[Young Thug]] and [[Wiz Khalifa]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=June 8, 2014 |title=Soulja Boy Says Lil Wayne Will Be Featured On His "King Soulja 3" Mixtape |url=http://www.lilwaynehq.com/2014/06/soulja-boy-says-lil-wayne-will-be-featured-on-his-king-soulja-3-mixtape/ |website=Lil Wayne Fansite - Weezy Blog}}</ref> On June 29, Way released ''King Soulja 3'' as his second digital album via iTunes, featuring appearances from [[Gudda Gudda]] and Rich The Kid.<ref>{{Cite web |date=July 29, 2014 |title=iTunes - Music - King Soulja{{nbsp}}3 by Soulja Boy Tell 'Em |url=https://music.apple.com/us/album/king-soulja-3/897181680 |website=iTunes}}</ref> On October 31, Way released his mixtape ''Young Millionaire'', featuring appearances from [[Sean Kingston]], Cap.1 and Rich The Kid.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Soulja Boy - Young Millionaire |url=http://www.datpiff.com/Soulja-Boy-Young-Millionaire-mixtape.662008.html |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141109123043/http://www.datpiff.com/Soulja-Boy-Young-Millionaire-mixtape.662008.html |archive-date=November 9, 2014 |access-date=November 7, 2014 |website=DatPiff}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=October 31, 2014 |title=Soulja Boy - Young Millionaire |url=http://www.hotnewhiphop.com/soulja-boy-young-millionaire-new-mixtape.115552.html |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141107210459/http://www.hotnewhiphop.com/soulja-boy-young-millionaire-new-mixtape.115552.html |archive-date=November 7, 2014 |access-date=November 7, 2014 |website=HotNewHipHop}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Meaghan Garvey |title=Listen to Soulja Boy's "Young Millionaire" Mixtape |url=http://www.complex.com/music/2014/11/soulja-boy-young-millionaire-mixtape |website=Complex}}</ref> On November 17, Way announced via his Instagram that he had signed a new label deal with [[Universal Music Group]] and also revealed the title, cover art and release date for his fourth studio album, ''[[Loyalty (Soulja Boy album)|Loyalty]]'', originally scheduled for release on December 2, 2014, but pushed back to February 3, 2015.<ref>{{Cite web |date=November 17, 2014 |title=Soulja Boy announces new album, ''Loyalty'', release date, and label deal [PHOTO] |url=http://www.hip-hopvibe.com/2014/11/17/soulja-boy-announces-new-album-loyalty-release-date-and-label-deal-photo/ |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141129024456/http://www.hip-hopvibe.com/2014/11/17/soulja-boy-announces-new-album-loyalty-release-date-and-label-deal-photo/ |archive-date=November 29, 2014 |website=Hip Hop Vibe}}</ref>

In 2015, following the release of his fourth studio album, ''Loyalty'', Way released additional singles, mixtapes and a digital album. For this upcoming digital album, ''King Soulja 4'', Way released the single "Whippin My Wrist (Too Rich)" on February 27; it charted on the ''Billboard'' [[Billboard Twitter Real-Time|Trending 140]] at number 48.<ref>{{Cite web |date=February 27, 2015 |title=iTunes - Music - Whippin My Wrist - Single by Soulja Boy Tell 'Em |url=https://itunes.apple.com/us/album/whippin-my-wrist-single/id972711476 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150402114251/https://itunes.apple.com/us/album/whippin-my-wrist-single/id972711476 |url-status=dead |archive-date=April 2, 2015 |access-date=July 20, 2015 |website=iTunes}}</ref><ref>{{Cite AV media |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5FuxnEg6mI4 |title=Soulja Boy Tell 'Em - Whippin' My Wrist |date=April 3, 2015 |access-date=July 20, 2015 |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/varchive/youtube/20211030/5FuxnEg6mI4 |archive-date=October 30, 2021 |via=YouTube}}{{cbignore}}</ref> On May 16, Way released his mixtape ''Swag The Mixtape'' featuring appearances from [[Migos]], [[Peewee Longway]], [[Troy Ave]], Johnny Cinco, [[Chief Keef]], Pack Strong, Paul Allen, Chella H, Calico Jonez and [[Kyle Massey]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Soulja Boy - Swag |url=http://www.datpiff.com/Soulja-Boy-Swag-mixtape.709530.html |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150721140947/http://www.datpiff.com/Soulja-Boy-Swag-mixtape.709530.html |archive-date=July 21, 2015 |access-date=July 20, 2015 |website=DatPiff}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Danny Schwartz |date=May 16, 2015 |title=Soulja Boy - Swag The Mixtape - Download and Listen [New Mixtape] |url=http://www.hotnewhiphop.com/soulja-boy-swag-the-mixtape-new-mixtape.115838.html |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150722053319/http://www.hotnewhiphop.com/soulja-boy-swag-the-mixtape-new-mixtape.115838.html |archive-date=July 22, 2015 |access-date=July 20, 2015 |website=HotNewHipHop}}</ref> On May 26, fellow Atlanta, Georgia artist [[Rich The Kid]] announced that he and Way would be releasing a collaboration album titled ''Get Rich''.<ref>{{Cite web |date=May 26, 2015 |title=Rich The Kid and Soulja Boy Are Dropping a Joint Album |url=http://www.xxlmag.com/news/2015/05/rich-the-kid-soulja-boy-joint-album/ |access-date=July 20, 2015 |website=XXL}}</ref> On June 3, via Twitter, Way revealed the title, cover art and release date to his upcoming fifth studio album, ''Blessed'', which was scheduled for release on July 28, 2015, but experienced several delays.<ref>{{Cite web |date=June 4, 2015 |title=Release Date: Soulja Boy - "Blessed" (Album) - Rap Direct |url=http://www.rapdirect.net/2015/06/news-soulja-boy-announces-new-album-title-release-date/ |access-date=July 20, 2015 |website=Rap Direct}}</ref> On June 30, Way released a mixtape titled ''25 The Movie''.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Soulja Boy - 25 The Movie |url=http://www.datpiff.com/Soulja-Boy-25-The-Movie-mixtape.725698.html |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150924050022/http://www.datpiff.com/Soulja-Boy-25-The-Movie-mixtape.725698.html |archive-date=September 24, 2015 |access-date=September 20, 2015 |website=DatPiff}}</ref> On July 17, Way released a new single titled "Actavis" featuring Migos, taken from his fourth digital album ''King Soulja 4''.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Danny Schwartz |date=July 17, 2015 |title=Soulja Boy - Actavis featuring Migos - Stream and Listen [New Song] |url=https://www.hotnewhiphop.com/573280-soulja-boy-activais-feat-migos-new-song |access-date=July 20, 2015 |website=HotNewHipHop}}</ref> On July 23, Way released his mixtape titled ''M & M: Money and Music''.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Soulja Boy Tell 'Em - M & M: Money & Music Hosted by SODMG, PTE |url=http://www.datpiff.com/Soulja-Boy-Tell-Em-M-M-Money-Music-mixtape.724418.html |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150910174701/htt