===2018–present: ''Breakfast Club'' interview and ''How Can You Blame Me?''===
[[File:Soulja-boy-concert-2019-january.PNG|thumb|right|220px|Soulja Boy performing at a concert in January 2019.|alt=]]
In late 2018, Way went on Instagram Live and responded to fan comments suggesting Tyga had the biggest comeback of any artist in 2018. Way angrily claimed he had had the biggest comeback, with a snippet of the video going viral that same day.<ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Lamarre |first=Carl |date=January 11, 2019 |title=Soulja Boy Calls Out Fans for Dubbing Tyga Comeback Artist of 2018: 'Get the F--- Out of Here' |url=https://www.billboard.com/music/rb-hip-hop/soulja-boy-tyga-instagram-video-8493166/ |access-date=August 22, 2019 |magazine=Billboard}}</ref> Later in early 2019, Way made an appearance on the radio show [[The Breakfast Club (radio show)|''The Breakfast Club'']], where he defended his statements. A snippet of Way responding to a question about fellow musician [[Drake (musician)|Drake]] became a viral meme following the interview.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Coleman |first=Vernon |date=January 19, 2019 |title=SOULJA BOY'S 'THE BREAKFAST CLUB' INTERVIEW SPARKS HILARIOUS MEMES |url=https://www.xxlmag.com/news/2019/01/soulja-boys-the-breakfast-club-interview-sparks-hilarious-memes/ |access-date=August 22, 2019 |publisher=XXL |quote=Soulja's sheer audacity, animated candor and facts to back up some of his claims, had the internet going nuts. So, of course, the memes emerged.}}</ref>

Way continued to release singles and two mix-tapes throughout 2019, releasing the first mix-tape, ''Fuego'', on January 10,<ref>{{Cite web |last=A |first=Aron |date=January 10, 2019 |title=Soulja Boy Is Back With "Fuego" Mixtape Ft. Lil Mosey |url=https://www.hotnewhiphop.com/137582-soulja-boy-is-back-with-fuego-mixtape-ft-lil-mosey-new-mixtape |access-date=August 22, 2019 |website=HotNewHipHop}}</ref> and the second mix-tape, ''Tell Ya'', on March 25.<ref>{{Cite news |last=A |first=Aron |date=March 25, 2019 |title=Soulja Boy Is Back With His New EP "Tell Ya" |url=https://www.hotnewhiphop.com/158600-soulja-boy-is-back-with-his-new-ep-tell-ya-new-mixtape |access-date=August 22, 2019 |work=HotNewHipHop}}</ref> Way announced on January 18, 2019, he was working on his fifth studio album titled ''[[How Can You Blame Me?]]'',<ref>{{Cite web |date=January 18, 2019 |title=Soulja Boy Hypes Himself, Announces New Album, Further Trashes Tyga & Drake |url=https://www.tmz.com/2019/01/18/soulja-boy-record-label-new-album-hottest-rapper-tyga-drake-beef/ |access-date=February 24, 2019 |website=TMZ}}</ref> (originally titled ''Blessed''), with the album set to be released on July 28. The album was delayed for unknown reasons following Way's 2019 incarceration.<ref name="Trammell-2019">{{Cite web |date=May 2019 |title=Soulja Boy sentenced to 240 days in jail for violating his probation |url=https://edition-m.cnn.com/2019/05/01/entertainment/soulja-boy-jail-sentencing-trnd}}</ref> On July 28, 2020, following his rising success as a [[Twitch (service)|Twitch]] streamer,<ref>{{Cite web |last=BET Staff |date=August 6, 2020 |title=Fans Are Getting Lit Watching Soulja Boy Play 'Call Of Duty' On Twitch |url=https://www.bet.com/article/nenkgd/fans-are-living-for-soulja-boy-s-call-of-duty-live-streams |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221201022658/https://www.bet.com/article/nenkgd/fans-are-living-for-soulja-boy-s-call-of-duty-live-streams |url-status=dead |archive-date=December 1, 2022 |access-date=October 20, 2020 |website=BET}}</ref> Way released the mixtape ''King Soulja 9'',<ref>{{Cite web |last=Mahjouri |first=Shakiel |date=July 27, 2020 |title=Soulja Boy Teases 'King Soulja 9' Album Release At Midnight |url=https://etcanada.com/news/673439/soulja-boy-teases-king-soulja-9-album-release-at-midnight/ |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200729212427/https://etcanada.com/news/673439/soulja-boy-teases-king-soulja-9-album-release-at-midnight/ |archive-date=July 29, 2020 |access-date=October 20, 2020 |website=ETCanada}}</ref> followed by several singles throughout 2020. In late November 2020, Way released the mixtape ''Swag 3'', and continued to release several singles such as ''Pandemic'' and ''CEO'' at the start of 2021.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Marie |first=Erika |date=November 30, 2020 |title=Soulja Boy Returns With 16-Track "Swag 3" Mixtape |url=https://www.hotnewhiphop.com/306341-soulja-boy-returns-with-16-track-swag-3-mixtape-new-mixtape |access-date=January 18, 2021 |website=HotNewHipHop}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Admin |date=January 4, 2021 |title=SOULJA BOY - PANDEMIC SONG |url=https://www.rapbasement.com/audio/soulja-boy-pandemic-song.html |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210507223547/https://www.rapbasement.com/audio/soulja-boy-pandemic-song.html |archive-date=May 7, 2021 |access-date=January 18, 2021 |website=rapbasement}}</ref> On February 1, 2021, Way released the mixtape ''Soulja World'', which features frequent collaborator [[Lil B]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Robinson |first=Joshua |date=February 2, 2021 |title=Soulja Boy Welcomes Listeners To "Soulja World" |url=https://www.hotnewhiphop.com/312498-soulja-boy-welcomes-listeners-to-soulja-world-new-mixtape |access-date=February 15, 2021 |website=HotNewHipHop}}</ref> In April 2021, Soulja Boy signed a record deal with [[Virgin Records|Virgin Music]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=April 9, 2021 |title=Soulja Boy Signs with Virgin Music |url=https://www.hotnewhiphop.com/soulja-boy-signs-with-virgin-music-news.129928.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210409155438/https://www.hotnewhiphop.com/soulja-boy-signs-with-virgin-music-news.129928.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=April 9, 2021 |website=HNHH}}</ref>

In 2021, Way publicly feuded with rapper [[Kanye West]] after his recorded verse was removed from the song "Remote Control" from West's album ''[[Donda]]'' without prior notice.<ref>{{cite web |title=Kanye West apologises to Soulja Boy for cutting his Donda verse |url=https://www.the-independent.com/arts-entertainment/music/news/kanye-west-soulja-boy-verse-apology-b1955682.html |website=The Independent |access-date=2026-02-19}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Soulja Boy calls Kanye West a coward for cutting him from Donda without warning |url=https://www.nme.com/news/music/soulja-boy-calls-kanye-west-a-coward-for-cutting-him-from-donda-without-warning-3033676 |website=NME |access-date=2026-02-19}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Soulja Boy demands apology from Kanye West for being left off of Donda |url=https://www.revolt.tv/article/2021-09-06/101607/soulja-boy-demands-apology-from-kanye-west-for-being-left-off-of-donda |website=REVOLT |access-date=2026-02-19}}</ref> West later apologised and the pair reconciled.<ref>{{cite web |title=Ye Apologizes to Soulja Boy For Leaving Him Off Donda Tracklist |url=https://www.billboard.com/music/music-news/ye-apologizes-soulja-boy-donda-tracklist-9657779/ |website=Billboard |access-date=2026-02-19}}</ref> In February 2022, Way appeared as a featured artist on West's album ''[[Donda 2]]''.<ref>{{cite web |title=Kanye West Premieres Donda 2 at Miami Event |url=https://www.billboard.com/music/rb-hip-hop/kanye-west-donda-2-album-miami-1235034811/ |website=Billboard |access-date=2026-02-19}}</ref> Behind-the-scenes footage later showed Way recording his verse at his home studio with producer Starquake shortly before the album's live performance in Miami.<ref>{{cite AV media |title=Soulja Boy & Ye: Turning Beef into Collaborating on Donda 2 The Life Of Draco |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bBdKZPni1tA&t=1024s |medium=YouTube video |publisher=REVOLT |date=2023-03-27 |access-date=2026-02-19 |time=17:02}}</ref>

Way performed at an event hosted by [[David Sacks]] celebrating the [[second inauguration of Donald Trump]] in January 2025, which sparked some backlash from the [[Left-wing politics|left-wing]]. Way defended his decision to perform at the event, stating that the Trump administration reached out to him and offered him money to perform, while [[Joe Biden]] and [[Kamala Harris]] did not.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Oatman |first=Alexis |date=2025-01-19 |title=Soulja Boy Fires Back At Critics Over Set At Trump's Crypto Ball |url=https://www.hot97.com/news/soulja-boy-fires-back-at-critics-over-set-at-trumps-crypto-ball/ |access-date=2025-12-26 |website=HOT 97 |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Inman |first=DeMicia |date=2025-01-21 |title=Soulja Boy Responds To Backlash After Performing At Crypto Ball: “Cancel Me All Day” |url=https://www.vibe.com/news/entertainment/soulja-boy-backlash-crypto-ball-cancel-me-1234978443/ |access-date=2025-12-26 |website=VIBE.com |language=en-US}}</ref> He further argued: "Trump wasn't even there. It wasn't even a Trump event. It was the Crypto Ball for crypto. And I'll go again." He would later admit he was "misled by the political nature of the event," according to ''[[Newsweek]]''.<ref>{{Cite news |title=Soulja Boy defends Trump inauguration party performance w... |url=https://www.newsweek.com/soulja-boy-defends-trump-inauguration-party-crypto-ball-snoop-dogg-2017406 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250918190406/https://www.newsweek.com/soulja-boy-defends-trump-inauguration-party-crypto-ball-snoop-dogg-2017406 |archive-date=2025-09-18 |access-date=2025-12-26 |work=Newsweek |language=en}}</ref>

==Musical style==
Speaking on his rudimentary rapping technique and [[wikt:vacuous|vacuous]] lyrical style, in a November 2010 interview with ''[[XXL Magazine]]'', while Way was explaining how he has grown as an [[rapper|emcee]] he stated:
<blockquote>I can't keep playin' around, because if you keep playin' around, people are gonna think you're a joke{{nbsp}}... At some point, you have to get serious. But don't get it twisted, Soulja doesn't wanna be the next [[Lupe Fiasco]]. I don't want to be super-Lupe-Fiasco-lyrical and niggas don't know what the fuck I'm talking about," he added. "I want to be the best," he continued. "I want to be recognized in that category where I'm nominated for Best Rapper with [[Jay-Z]] and [[Kanye West|Kanye]] and [[Lil Wayne|Wayne]]. I wanna get a [[Grammy]]. I want Best Rap Album of the Year{{nbsp}}... I've made millions of dollars off of doing my style, but, hey, I can rap, too.</blockquote>

On December 5, 2010, Way clarified this statement that Lupe Fiasco was too "lyrical", explaining that he wanted to keep the rudimentary music style he developed and justified why he doesn't keep a lyrically based flow and more substantially profound lyrical subject matter on every song.<ref>{{Cite web |date=July 20, 2015 |title=SOHH.com - Soulja Boy On Lupe Fiasco "Super Lyrical" Remarks, "I Don't Even Care, 'Cause Controversy Sells" |url=http://www.sohh.com/2010/12/soulja_boy_on_lupe_fiasco_super_lyrical.html |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140712162454/http://www.sohh.com/2010/12/soulja_boy_on_lupe_fiasco_super_lyrical.html |archive-date=July 12, 2014 |access-date=July 20, 2015 |website=SOHH.com}}</ref> According to Way:

<blockquote>[''XXL Magazine''] didn't [publish] my whole statement. And basically, the interviewer dude, he was asking me why do I make lyrical songs like 'Only God Knows,' 'Born'{{nbsp}}... 'The World So Cold,' and why do I make non-lyrical songs like '[[Crank That (Soulja Boy)|Crank That]]' and '[[Pretty Boy Swag]]'? And he was like, why don't I just be lyrical all the time? And I was telling him on [a] specific song, like 'Pretty Boy Swag,' I ain't wanna be all lyrical. I just wanted to be straight-up, and just be simple, [so] people can get what I'm saying 'cause it's a club song. But I wasn't saying as far as my whole music [output, with] all of my songs, that I don't wanna be rappin' like Lupe Fiasco.</blockquote>

Way further clarified there was no animosity between him and Fiasco and that the two had planned to record a song together.<ref>{{Cite web |date=December 2010 |title=Soulja Boy Plans to Work with Lupe Fiasco |url=https://www.xxlmag.com/soulja-boy-plans-to-work-with-lupe-fiasco/}}</ref>

Way's music has been banned from some school dances{{which|date=July 2024}} for alleged sexual or violent content and/or innuendos.{{citation needed|date=July 2024}} In the original YouTube video for "Shootout", Way demonstrates his dance while holding a handgun in each hand and pretending to shoot into the audience.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Harrington |first=Richard |date=December 21, 2007 |title=Soulja Boy Cranks It Up Big-Time |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/wp-dyn/content/article/2007/12/20/AR2007122000913.html |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |page=WE12 |quote=...{{nbsp}}some school dances and skating rinks have banned the song for sexual slang and innuendo, though naive teens seem to be more interested in the dance and the beat than in the lyrics.}}</ref>

